      "math_academy_id": "e-18931",
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{10} - \\sqrt{6}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{10} + \\sqrt{6},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6}{\\sqrt{10}-\\sqrt{6}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}}{\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{(\\sqrt{10})^{2}-(\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{10-6} & = \\\\ \\frac{6(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(\\sqrt{10}+\\sqrt{6})}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
